```js
const orders = [
  { id: 1042, customer: 'M. Alinejad', total: 129, status: 'paid' },
  { id: 1043, customer: 'K. Smith', total: 59, status: 'pending' },
  { id: 1044, customer: 'J. Doe', total: 249, status: 'refunded' }
];
const rows = document.getElementById('rows');
rows.innerHTML = orders.map(o => `
  <tr><td>#${o.id}</td><td>${o.customer}</td><td>$${o.total}</td>
  <td><span class='pill ${o.status}'>${o.status}</span></td></tr>`).join('');
```

Why it works: rows are rendered by mapping the data array to HTML, so adding an order means adding one object; the status class is injected into the pill, letting CSS own all the color decisions.

