```css
/* Option A: @import inside your stylesheet — works, but slower */
@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;700&display=swap');

body { font-family: 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif; }
```

```html
<!-- Option B: link in the document head — preferred -->
<link rel='preconnect' href='https://fonts.googleapis.com'>
<link rel='preconnect' href='https://fonts.gstatic.com' crossorigin>
<link href='https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;700&display=swap' rel='stylesheet'>
```

Why it works: @import is only discovered after the browser downloads and parses your CSS file, which serializes the requests — CSS file, then font CSS, then font files (a waterfall). The link tag is found immediately when the HTML head is parsed, so the font CSS starts downloading in parallel with your own stylesheets. On slow connections the @import pattern can delay first paint by hundreds of milliseconds. Use @import only in quick prototypes; in production always use link (or bundle/self-host the fonts).

```html
