```css
.scene {
  min-height: 100vh;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  background: linear-gradient(120deg, #7c3aed, #db2777, #0ea5e9);
}
.glass {
  width: 320px;
  padding: 2rem;
  border-radius: 16px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.12);
  backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(160%);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(160%);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.25);
  color: #fff;
  box-shadow: 0 8px 32px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25);
}
```

Why it works: backdrop-filter blurs whatever is painted *behind* the element, so the card picks up the colors of the gradient behind it while staying translucent — that is the entire glass effect. The 1px semi-transparent border mimics the bright edge of real glass and separates the card from the background. The -webkit- prefix is still needed for Safari, and the effect only reads well when something colorful sits behind the card; on a plain white page it is invisible.

```css
.card {
  background: #fff;
  border-radius: 12px;
  box-shadow:
    0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06),
    0 4px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08),
    0 12px 32px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.10);
}
```

Why it works: real shadows are not one shadow — they are an ambient shadow (light from all directions, tight and faint) plus a key shadow (from above, larger and softer). Stacking three box-shadows from tight to wide with very low alpha values reproduces that falloff, which the eye reads as soft and physical. The common mistake is a single shadow like 0 4px 10px rgba(0,0,0,.5): high opacity with small blur equals harsh. Two rules of thumb: large blur + low alpha, and never pure black — a slightly tinted color like rgba(15,23,42,.12) looks even more natural.

