```js
const items = ['Mouse', 'Keyboard', 'Monitor', 'Webcam', 'Microphone'];
const results = document.getElementById('results');

function debounce(fn, delay) {
  let timer;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), delay);
  };
}

const render = query => {
  const hits = items.filter(i => i.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase()));
  results.innerHTML = hits.map(h => '<li>' + h + '</li>').join('');
};

document.getElementById('q').addEventListener('input',
  debounce(e => render(e.target.value.trim()), 250)
);
render('');
```

Why it works: the input event fires on every keystroke — typing 'monitor' triggers 7 renders. The debounce closure holds one timer variable; each new call clears the pending timeout and schedules the work 250ms in the future, so only after the user pauses does fn actually run. That single timer slot is what makes debounce different from throttle (which runs at most once per interval): for search-as-you-type you want to react to the last keystroke, not intermediate ones. The closure is also why each debounced function keeps its own independent timer. 250-300ms is the sweet spot — long enough to batch typing, short enough to feel instant.

