```js
/* IntersectionObserver version — only when you need custom behavior,
   e.g. swapping data-src placeholders or adding a fade-in on load */
const io = new IntersectionObserver((entries, obs) => {
  entries.forEach(en => {
    if (!en.isIntersecting) return;
    const img = en.target;
    img.src = img.dataset.src;
    img.addEventListener('load', () => img.classList.add('loaded'), { once: true });
    obs.unobserve(img);
  });
}, { rootMargin: '200px 0px' });
document.querySelectorAll('img[data-src]').forEach(img => io.observe(img));
```

Why it works: loading='lazy' is now supported in all evergreen browsers and uses the browser's own viewport distance heuristics — it should be your default. The width and height attributes are essential: they let the browser reserve space before the image arrives, preventing layout shift (CLS). rootMargin 200px in the IO version starts the fetch slightly before the image scrolls into view, which hides the network latency. The rule of thumb: native lazy loading for everything below the fold, and eager + preload + fetchpriority=high for the LCP hero image — lazy-loading your largest visible image actively hurts your LCP score.

