```js
const TARGET = new Date('2025-12-31T23:59:59');
const els = {
  d: document.getElementById('d'),
  h: document.getElementById('h'),
  m: document.getElementById('m'),
  s: document.getElementById('s')
};
const pad = n => String(n).padStart(2, '0');

function tick() {
  const diff = TARGET - Date.now();
  if (diff <= 0) {
    Object.values(els).forEach(el => (el.textContent = '00'));
    clearInterval(handle);
    return;
  }
  els.d.textContent = pad(Math.floor(diff / 86400000));
  els.h.textContent = pad(Math.floor(diff / 3600000) % 24);
  els.m.textContent = pad(Math.floor(diff / 60000) % 60);
  els.s.textContent = pad(Math.floor(diff / 1000) % 60);
}
const handle = setInterval(tick, 1000);
tick();
```

Why it works: all arithmetic derives from one number — milliseconds remaining — and the modulo operations split it into days, hours, minutes, seconds without drift. Calling tick() once immediately means the user never sees the placeholder 00 for a second. The diff <= 0 branch is the piece people forget: it pins the display to zero and clears the interval, so the timer stops consuming CPU after expiry instead of counting negative numbers forever. If you need sub-second precision or timer accuracy under throttled background tabs, compute the remaining time from Date.now() each tick (as done here) rather than decrementing a counter — setInterval fires late under throttling, but the target-date math self-corrects.

