```js
const status = document.getElementById('status');
const list = document.getElementById('list');
const error = document.getElementById('error');

function setLoading(on) {
  status.hidden = !on;
  list.hidden = true;
  error.hidden = true;
}

async function load() {
  setLoading(true);
  try {
    const res = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users');
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
    const users = await res.json();
    list.innerHTML = users.map(u => '<li>' + u.name + '</li>').join('');
    list.hidden = false;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    error.hidden = false;
  } finally {
    status.hidden = true;
  }
}

document.getElementById('retry').addEventListener('click', load);
load();
```

Why it works: every request has exactly three states — loading, success, error — and the pattern makes each one explicit instead of leaving the UI stuck. The finally block hides the spinner no matter which path ran, which is the classic bug in hand-rolled fetch code. Checking res.ok matters: fetch only rejects on network failure, so a 404 or 500 arrives as a resolved promise and must be thrown manually to reach the catch branch. Rendering escapes user-controlled strings safely here because the API data is simple, but for untrusted input you would swap innerHTML for createElement/textContent. The retry button reuses the same load() function, so the state machine stays single-sourced.
